{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":482757,"name":"Avi Schwartz","username":"LLMTester","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/l/df788c/{size}.png","created_at":"2026-09-04T02:08:10.945Z","cooked":"\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-where-does-the-bug-appear-featureproduct-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-where-does-the-bug-appear-featureproduct-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eWhere does the bug appear (feature/product)?\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGrok Bot\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-describe-the-bug-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-describe-the-bug-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eDescribe the Bug\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTwo separate Grok Bot chats/bots are using the same underlying agent for different long-running research projects. Project A is running in one bot/chat, while Project B is running in a separate bot/chat.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBoth projects require regular \u003cstrong\u003estatus beats\u003c/strong\u003e every few minutes, reporting what was found and including a link.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe problem: progress from one project shows up in the other bot/chat. Because both bots share the same underlying agent, durable memory and conversation history contain information from both projects. A status beat for Project B keeps Project B “hot,” so the next turn in the separate bot/chat often answers with Project B findings instead of the findings for that bot’s own project.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe two bots are separate conversation surfaces, but they are backed by the same agent ID. The two projects can also have separate disk folders; the mix-up still occurs within the agent’s shared memory/history.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-steps-to-reproduce-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-steps-to-reproduce-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eSteps to Reproduce\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eCreate two separate Grok Bot chats/bots that use the same underlying agent.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRun Project A in Bot/Chat A.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRun Project B in Bot/Chat B.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eGive both projects recurring status-beat routines that run every few minutes and report findings with links.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eAllow several Project B status beats to run, refreshing Project B in the shared agent memory/history.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eIn Bot/Chat A, ask for Project A status, links, or an ambiguous request such as “continue.”\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eObserve: Bot/Chat A receives Project B content instead of Project A content.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-expected-behavior-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-expected-behavior-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eExpected Behavior\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEach separate bot/chat should remain isolated to its own project, even when both are backed by the same underlying agent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA status beat generated for Project B should not contaminate, refresh, or prime the context for Project A.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDurable memory and conversation history should be scoped to the individual bot/chat or project, rather than globally shared across separate bots using the same agent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-actual-behavior-5\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-actual-behavior-5\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eActual Behavior\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBot/Chat A receives Project B digests.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe underlying agent’s durable memory/history contains both projects as standing facts. Recurring beats for Project B make the leak worse by continually refreshing Project B’s context, making it more likely to appear in the next turn of Bot/Chat A.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe two bots are separate, but they are sharing the same agent and therefore sharing the agent’s memory/history.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-operating-system-6\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-operating-system-6\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eOperating System\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWindows 11\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-version-information-7\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-version-information-7\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eVersion Information\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNot provided\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-for-ai-issues-add-request-id-with-privacy-disabled-8\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-for-ai-issues-add-request-id-with-privacy-disabled-8\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eFor AI issues: add Request ID with privacy disabled\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eShared underlying agent: \u003ccode\u003e92b2d70e-b956-44b5-945b-849762ebb0a5\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBot/Chat A: Project A\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBot/Chat B: Project B\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-additional-information-9\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-additional-information-9\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eAdditional Information\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe two projects can use completely separate disk folders. The issue is not caused by shared project files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue occurs because separate Grok Bot chats/bots are backed by the same underlying agent, causing durable memory and conversation history to be shared between otherwise separate projects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRecurring status beats amplify the issue because each Project B beat refreshes Project B’s information in the shared agent context.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-ask-10\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-ask-10\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eAsk\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eScope durable memory and conversation history to the individual bot/chat or project, even when multiple separate bots use the same underlying agent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere should be a hard context boundary so that activity, memory, status beats, and findings from one bot/project cannot be used to answer another bot/project.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-482757-does-this-stop-you-from-using-cursor-11\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-482757-does-this-stop-you-from-using-cursor-11\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eDoes this stop you from using Cursor?\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNo — but running two separate research projects through different bots that share the same agent is unsafe without manual isolation.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-04T07:44:52.750Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":21,"reads":7,"readers_count":6,"score":106.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":170523,"topic_slug":"status-beat-leaks-between-bots","display_username":"Avi Schwartz","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":4,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":136058,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":false,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/status-beat-leaks-between-bots/170523/1","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":482921,"name":"Colin","username":"Colin","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/forum.cursor.com/colin/{size}/90452_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-04T07:44:42.332Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eHey \u003ca class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/llmtester\"\u003e@LLMTester\u003c/a\u003e, thanks for the detailed report.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat you are seeing is how a Grok Bot agent works today, rather than something broken in your setup!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEach bot has one conversation and one memory, and that single history spans both its 1:1 chat with you and every group it is a member of. Group turns are tagged internally so the bot knows which room it is speaking in, but it is expected to draw on everything it knows, including its 1:1 history, and its saved memories belong to the bot, not to a chat. That means two long running projects on the same bot will cross over, exactly as you describe.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe reliable way to keep Project X and Project Y separate right now is to give each project its own bot. If you want to stay on one bot, you can also ask it to keep Project Y notes in files under its project folder instead of saving them to memory, but that relies on the model following the instruction, so separate bots are the safer option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eChat scoped memory is something the team is actively working toward!\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-04T07:44:42.332Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":4,"readers_count":3,"score":0.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":170523,"topic_slug":"status-beat-leaks-between-bots","display_username":"Colin","primary_group_name":"CursorStaff","flair_name":"CursorStaff","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/cursor1/original/3X/a/d/ad08adc640929ae1e98be69dab1ad0844cee99a0.png","flair_bg_color":"","flair_color":"","flair_group_id":42,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"Community Suppo